1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,379
Welcome to Unit Seven of Algebra Two:
radical functions. We start with nth roots

2
00:00:06,379 --> 00:00:08,213
and rational exponents.

3
00:00:09,333 --> 00:00:13,707
The nth root of a is a number that, raised
to the n, gives a.

4
00:00:14,157 --> 00:00:18,965
The cube root of sixty-four is four,
because four cubed is sixty-four. The

5
00:00:18,965 --> 00:00:21,239
fourth root of eighty-one is three.

6
00:00:22,367 --> 00:00:27,828
Odd roots of negatives are fine: the cube
root of negative eight is negative two.

7
00:00:28,278 --> 00:00:33,923
Even roots of negatives aren't real
numbers. And for even n, the root of x to

8
00:00:33,923 --> 00:00:36,342
the n is the absolute value of x.

9
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:43,959
A rational exponent combines a root and a
power: a to the m over n is the nth root

10
00:00:43,959 --> 00:00:45,701
of a, raised to the m.

11
00:00:46,151 --> 00:00:51,255
So thirty-two to the three fifths is the
fifth root of thirty-two, which is two,

12
00:00:51,255 --> 00:00:52,658
cubed, which is eight.

13
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:55,805
All the exponent rules still work.

14
00:00:56,255 --> 00:01:02,466
x to the one half times x to the one third
is x to the five sixths. And x to the two

15
00:01:02,466 --> 00:01:04,831
thirds, all cubed, is x squared.

16
00:01:05,967 --> 00:01:11,129
You can move between forms. The cube root
of x squared is x to the two thirds.

17
00:01:11,579 --> 00:01:16,657
And y to the negative three fourths is one
over the fourth root of y cubed.

18
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:21,512
Your turn. Evaluate sixteen to the
negative three fourths.

19
00:01:21,962 --> 00:01:23,498
Pause the video and try it.

20
00:01:23,948 --> 00:01:28,998
The fourth root of sixteen is two, and two
cubed is eight. The negative exponent

21
00:01:28,998 --> 00:01:30,071
gives one eighth.

22
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:36,971
In this lesson, we'll simplify radicals of
any index, and add, multiply and divide

23
00:01:36,971 --> 00:01:37,323
them.

24
00:01:38,467 --> 00:01:41,581
Look for factors that are perfect nth
powers.

25
00:01:42,031 --> 00:01:47,438
The cube root of fifty-four is the cube
root of twenty-seven times two, which is

26
00:01:47,438 --> 00:01:49,669
three times the cube root of two.

27
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:55,195
Radicals with the same index multiply and
divide under one root.

28
00:01:55,645 --> 00:02:01,546
The cube root of four times the cube root
of ten is the cube root of forty, which is

29
00:02:01,546 --> 00:02:03,794
two times the cube root of five.

30
00:02:04,933 --> 00:02:08,539
Add only like radicals: same index, same
radicand.

31
00:02:08,989 --> 00:02:14,130
Two cube root of three plus five cube root
of three is seven cube root of three.

32
00:02:15,267 --> 00:02:20,493
To rationalize a binomial denominator,
multiply by its conjugate.

33
00:02:20,943 --> 00:02:26,613
Four over three minus root five: multiply
by three plus root five. The bottom

34
00:02:26,613 --> 00:02:32,357
becomes nine minus five, which is four. So
the answer is three plus root five.

35
00:02:33,500 --> 00:02:38,023
For a cube root in the denominator,
multiply to make a perfect cube.

36
00:02:38,473 --> 00:02:44,542
One over the cube root of two: multiply by
the cube root of four over itself. The

37
00:02:44,542 --> 00:02:47,689
answer is the cube root of four, over two.

38
00:02:48,833 --> 00:02:52,908
Your turn. Simplify the cube root of
eighty-one x to the fourth.

39
00:02:53,358 --> 00:02:54,894
Pause the video and try it.

40
00:02:55,344 --> 00:03:01,500
Eighty-one x to the fourth is twenty-seven
x cubed times three x. So it's three x

41
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:03,856
times the cube root of three x.

42
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,841
In this lesson, we'll solve equations with
radicals, and learn why checking is

43
00:03:09,841 --> 00:03:10,461
essential.

44
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:15,803
Isolate the radical, then raise both sides
to the index's power.

45
00:03:16,253 --> 00:03:20,626
For square roots, square both sides. For
cube roots, cube both sides.

46
00:03:21,767 --> 00:03:25,713
Solve the square root of x plus three,
plus two, equals seven.

47
00:03:26,163 --> 00:03:31,760
Isolate: the root of x plus three equals
five. Square: x plus three equals

48
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:34,483
twenty-five, so x equals twenty-two.

49
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:41,659
Squaring can create extraneous solutions.
Solve the root of x plus six equals x.

50
00:03:42,109 --> 00:03:48,996
Square: x plus six equals x squared, so x
squared minus x minus six equals zero. x

51
00:03:48,996 --> 00:03:51,431
equals three or negative two.

52
00:03:51,881 --> 00:03:57,363
Check negative two: the root of four is
two, not negative two. So only x equals

53
00:03:57,363 --> 00:03:58,196
three works.

54
00:03:59,333 --> 00:04:04,411
With two radicals, isolate one, square,
then isolate the other and square again.

55
00:04:04,861 --> 00:04:10,960
The root of x plus five equals one plus
the root of x gives x equals four. Check:

56
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:13,671
three equals one plus two. It works.

57
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:19,003
For rational exponents, raise both sides
to the reciprocal power.

58
00:04:19,453 --> 00:04:25,529
x to the two thirds equals nine: raise to
the three halves. x equals plus or minus

59
00:04:25,529 --> 00:04:26,493
twenty-seven.

60
00:04:27,633 --> 00:04:31,900
Your turn. Solve the cube root of two x
minus one equals three.

61
00:04:32,350 --> 00:04:33,886
Pause the video and try it.

62
00:04:34,336 --> 00:04:40,181
Cube both sides: two x minus one equals
twenty-seven. So x equals fourteen.

63
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:46,463
In this last lesson of Unit Seven, we'll
graph square root and cube root functions.

64
00:04:47,600 --> 00:04:53,543
y equals root x starts at the origin and
passes through one comma one, four comma

65
00:04:53,543 --> 00:04:55,451
two, and nine comma three.

66
00:04:55,901 --> 00:04:57,821
It rises more and more slowly.

67
00:04:58,967 --> 00:05:04,961
The same transformations apply. y equals
two root of x minus one, plus three,

68
00:05:04,961 --> 00:05:09,164
starts at one comma three, and rises twice
as steeply.

69
00:05:09,614 --> 00:05:11,854
A negative in front flips it downward.

70
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,755
y equals the cube root of x passes through
negative eight comma negative two,

71
00:05:18,755 --> 00:05:24,285
negative one comma negative one, zero, one
comma one, and eight comma two.

72
00:05:24,735 --> 00:05:28,959
It extends in both directions, and
flattens out near the origin.

73
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:35,704
The domain of a square root function is
where the inside is at least zero. For

74
00:05:35,704 --> 00:05:38,505
root of x minus one, x is at least one.

75
00:05:38,955 --> 00:05:43,371
Cube root functions have all real numbers
as their domain and range.

76
00:05:44,500 --> 00:05:50,367
The square root function is the inverse of
y equals x squared, with x at least zero.

77
00:05:50,817 --> 00:05:54,571
Their graphs are reflections across the
line y equals x.

78
00:05:55,700 --> 00:06:01,581
Your turn. Find the domain, range and
starting point of y equals negative root

79
00:06:01,581 --> 00:06:03,465
of x plus four, plus one.

80
00:06:03,915 --> 00:06:05,451
Pause the video and try it.

81
00:06:05,901 --> 00:06:12,116
It starts at negative four comma one.
Domain: x at least negative four. Range: y

82
00:06:12,116 --> 00:06:13,048
at most one.

83
00:06:14,167 --> 00:06:18,840
That completes Unit Seven. Next, we'll
study exponential and logarithmic

84
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:24,098
functions in Unit Eight. If this helped,
subscribe to School Math, Explained, and

85
00:06:24,098 --> 00:06:27,863
turn on notifications so you don't miss
